complexes, we reasoned that targeting Cr- and Tisubstituted MFU- $_{\cdot4}l$ would lead to efficient olefin polymerization catalysts.
## Results and Discussion
To incorporate Ti and Cr, we initially evaluated cation exchange under conditions successful for the incorporation of late transition metals into MFU- $_{\cdot4}l$ . However, treatment of MFU- $_{4}l$ with the more oxophilic and generally more air-sensitive titanium and chromium halides was unsuccessful at elevated temperature, leading to decomposition of the MOF or the metal precursors. The exchanges were successful, however, at room temperature, where various amounts of ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ , or $\mathrm{Ti}^{4^{+}}$ readily exchanged zinc ions in MFU- $\mathbf{\nabla}_{4}l.$ , as determined by inductively-coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES, Table 1). Thus, soaking solid MFU- $_{4}l$ in a DMF solution with excess $\mathrm{CrCl}_{3}(\mathrm{THF})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and catalytic ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{2+}$ replaces all four exchangeable $Z\mathbf{n}$ sites in every SBU to
